# Induction

**Induction** is the inference from observations to a general hypothesis. In
this vault it is never treated as a standalone operation: it is one half of a
cycle whose other half is [[deduction]]. Induction proposes a model, deduction
unfolds what the model implies, and testing those implications produces the
observations for the next induction.

The word names two different things, and this card keeps them in two lanes.

| | Induction as inference | Mathematical induction |
|---|---|---|
| Goes from | observations | a base case and a step |
| Arrives at | a hypothesis that may be false | a theorem about every natural number |
| Kind of operation | discovery, outside any fixed theory | proof, inside a fixed theory |
| Partner | [[deduction]], in a cycle | [[recursion]], as its definitional twin |
| Card | this one | [[mathematical-induction]] |

## Description

### Lane one: induction is always done in the context of deduction

A hypothesis reached by induction has no content until something is deduced
from it. "All observed cases fit this rule" becomes a claim about the world
only when the rule is used to derive a case not yet observed. Conversely,
deduction cannot start itself: it needs premises, and for anything that is not
already a formal theory those premises were reached by induction or
[[abduction]]. Neither operation does work alone.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[Observations] -->|Induction / abduction| B[Hypotheses or model]
 B -->|Deduction| C[Predictions]
 C -->|Testing| D[New observations]
 D --> A
```

This is the [[reasoning-cycle]]. Induction is its upward arc, from data to
model. Deduction is its downward arc, from model to consequences.

The pairing shows up in the discussion in three more guises.

- **Discovering a formalization versus executing one.** A conventional
  program executes an established formalization: given the rules, the result
  follows. That is the deductive arc. Work with a language model is useful
  where the rules are not yet fixed and have to be found or revised. That is
  the inductive arc. The practical line between the two kinds of tool runs
  here, and not between computable and uncomputable problems.
- **Adding axioms.** Inside a theory everything is deduction. When a theory
  leaves a statement undecided, as incompleteness guarantees it will, no
  deduction inside the theory settles it. A mathematician who adopts a new
  axiom is taking a step that is not deductive relative to that theory, and
  deduction then resumes inside the larger one. The sequence of theories built
  this way is a [[reflection-progression]]. Nothing guarantees that such a
  step preserves truth or consistency, which is exactly the mark of an
  inductive step.
- **Compression.** To discover a regularity in observations is to find a
  shorter description of them, and a shorter description is a model that
  predicts. Induction is the step labelled "discover regularities, build
  model" in the cycle that links compression to prediction.

Induction was missing from the first table of operations drawn up in the
discussion and was put back on request. The corrected placement is with
deduction and abduction, as one family of inference, and not with
generalization or summarization.

Two distinctions follow from that placement.

- **Induction is not generalization.** Induction is an inference that rests on
  evidence. [[generalization]] widens a concept or a claim, as when vector
  spaces are extended to modules, and may involve no evidence at all.
- **Induction is not abduction.** Induction goes from many cases to a rule.
  Abduction goes from one surprising case to a candidate explanation.

When a language model continues a pattern from examples it performs something
that resembles induction, but the result is unchecked. A system that offers
induction as an operation should give it a type, with examples as input and
hypotheses as output, and attach to each result its evidence and a confidence.
The discussion writes the signature as `Induce: Examples -> Hypotheses`,
beside `Deduce: (Theory, Premises) -> Conclusions`.

### Lane two: mathematical induction is another concept

Proving a statement for zero, and proving that it passes from each number to
the next, establishes it for every natural number. The conclusion is certain,
and nothing is observed. Mathematical induction is therefore a deductive proof
method, and belongs on the deduction side of the cycle above. It has its own
card, [[mathematical-induction]].

It sits in a different part of the map from lane one. Its relatives are
[[recursion]] and iteration: the mechanical, step-by-step constructions such
as repeated differentiation or the passage from addition to multiplication to
exponentiation. Lane one's relatives are the operations of inquiry.

## Relations

- It works only in a cycle with [[deduction]] {relation: contrasts}: induction supplies the model, deduction its consequences.
- It is the upward arc of the [[reasoning-cycle]] {relation: part-of}.
- It shares that arc with [[abduction]] {relation: contrasts}: many cases to a rule, against one case to an explanation.
- It is a different concept from [[mathematical-induction]] {relation: distinguished-from}, which is deductive.
- It is not [[generalization]] {relation: distinguished-from}: an inference from evidence, against a widening of scope.
- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}, restored to the list after being left out.
- It is what [[compression-and-prediction]] {relation: uses} describes as discovering regularities.
- It is the kind of step by which a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: example-of} acquires each new axiom.
- Its results are judged by [[evaluation]] {relation: uses} and corrected by [[revision]] {relation: uses}.
- As an operation offered by an agent it should be a [[typed-transformation]] {relation: is-a}.
- It bears on the question [[ai-oriented-language]] {relation: answers}: discovering a formalization is the inductive half of programming.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[deduction]].** Inference from premises and rules to what must follow.
  Seen from this card it is the half of the cycle that gives an induced
  hypothesis something to be tested on.
- **[[mathematical-induction]].** The proof scheme of base case and step. It
  shares the name and nothing else: its conclusions are certain and it lives
  inside a formal theory.
- **[[reasoning-cycle]].** Observations, hypothesis, predictions, test,
  repeated. It is the smallest unit in which induction makes sense.
- **[[reflection-progression]].** A sequence of theories, each extending the
  last by an axiom the last could not decide. Each extension is a step no
  deduction in the earlier theory could have made.
- **[[compression-and-prediction]].** The link between how well a model
  predicts and how short a code it yields. Induction is the model-building
  step of that link.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[induction]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[induction]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[classify-operation]] labels a passage
with the cognitive operation it performs and would use lane one of this card
as the definition of the label `induction`.

